## RFC 9180 (HPKE) — the published test vectors for every mode and suite
## this build implements, reproduced exactly.
##
## ## Where the vectors come from, and why that is the whole point
##
## A test vector generated by the implementation under test proves
## nothing: it will agree with a stable wrong answer forever. So none of
## the bytes below were produced by this repository. `RfcAppendixA` is a
## VERBATIM copy of Appendix A.1 and A.2 of RFC 9180 as published by the
## RFC Editor, wrapped hex and section headings included, taken from
##
##   https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9180.txt
##   sha256 f45a8b7c30231b26d668b1e8168074e5ab514b174d656b67e859895117f1f8f6
##
## lines 2096-2891 of that file, which is A.1 through the end of A.2. It
## is embedded rather than transcribed so there is no transcription step
## to get wrong, and it is PARSED rather than hand-copied into Nim
## literals so that a vector cannot be quietly dropped while re-typing.
##
## Because a parser can silently skip what it does not understand — and a
## fixture set that is not constrained by its own gate is a fixture set
## that can shrink to nothing — the parser counts what it refused to
## parse and the suite asserts that count is zero, asserts the number of
## setups, encryptions and exported values it recovered, and asserts the
## exact number of RFC values it compared against. A dropped vector moves
## one of those four numbers.
##
## ## The primitives are pinned independently
##
## The composition is checked against RFC 9180; the primitives it is
## built on are checked against their own documents, so a broken
## primitive cannot be hidden by a composition that consistently
## mis-uses it:
##
##   * HKDF-SHA256 — RFC 5869 Appendix A.1, A.2 and A.3, the three
##     SHA-256 cases, `PRK` and `OKM` both.
##     https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5869.txt
##     sha256 7a40eb3835b35fc947eb12a2ed614db079d43b26e50dbc537c31fba16397089c
##   * X25519 — RFC 7748 §5.2 (both vectors) and §6.1 (the Diffie-Hellman
##     vector, both directions, plus each party's public key from the
##     base point).
##     https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7748.txt
##     sha256 279ca0ecc5e92e2962e27b846986aeb74729d9dd34bd4a04a362f80dcb596ad3
##
## RFC 7748 §5.2 matters more than it looks. BearSSL takes the curve25519
## *scalar* big-endian and the *u-coordinate* little-endian; getting that
## backwards produces a perfectly well-formed wrong 32 bytes, and these
## two vectors are what says which way round it is.
##
## ## What this gate does NOT cover
##
## RFC 9180's Appendix A has SEVEN sub-appendices, A.1 through A.7. This
## gate uses two. A.3, A.4 and A.5 are DHKEM(P-256, HKDF-SHA256) — with
## HKDF-SHA256 or HKDF-SHA512, and AES-128-GCM or ChaCha20Poly1305 — A.6
## is DHKEM(P-521, HKDF-SHA512) with HKDF-SHA512 and AES-256-GCM, and A.7
## is the export-only AEAD over the same KEM and KDF this build does
## implement. Appendix A publishes NO P-384 and NO HKDF-SHA384 vectors at
## all. None of those five is implemented, none is nameable through this
## module's enums, and none is claimed here. What is claimed is the row that IS implemented —
## DHKEM(X25519, HKDF-SHA256) with HKDF-SHA256 and either AES-128-GCM or
## ChaCha20Poly1305 — in all four modes, which is exactly A.1 and A.2.
##
## ## Mocking
##
## None. Every byte below is either published by an RFC or computed by
## the code under test.

